+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012dTQdcT1k6rrfUoBmaJ1q4","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 90 spec tests pass. The `tests/tmp-adv*.test.ts` files are untracked artifacts created during this session's timeframe by another agent (not by me — I only used `/workspace/.probe/`, now deleted). They contain 12 failing assertions; worth flagging to the parent agent since `npm test` currently reports failures because of them.\n\nHere are my findings.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe implementation is well-structured: a single source of truth (`tasks` map), per-queue state objects, a comparator-based `dequeue`, and a `seq` counter for stable FIFO. Overload handling in `enqueue`, the `dependenciesSatisfied` check folded into `isAvailable`, and clearing the shutdown timer on early resolve are all done correctly. Cron DST rollover via `setHours`/`setDate` is genuinely correct (verified under `TZ=America/New_York` across both spring-forward and fall-back), and the retry backoff sequence on the normal dequeue→fail path is exact (`100, 200, 400, 800, 1600` for `backoffMs: 100`).\n\nThat said, there are several real correctness bugs, two of which are unbounded/permanent state corruption.\n\n**Environment note first:** `/workspace/tests/tmp-adv2.test.ts`, `tmp-adv3.test.ts`, `tmp-adversarial.test.ts` are untracked leftovers (not mine) that make `npm test` report **12 failed / 237 total**. The 12 real phase suites pass 90/90. These stray files should be deleted before anyone reads `npm test` output as a signal.\n\n---\n\n## HIGH\n\n### H1. `dequeue` ordering contradicts the phase-04 spec (priority and availability precedence are swapped)\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:294-300` (`comparePending`), used at `:269`.\n\nphase-04.md states: *\"When multiple delayed tasks are available, they are ordered by their availability time (earliest first), then by priority, then FIFO.\"* The comparator sorts priority first, then `availableAt`, then `seq` — the documented first two keys are inverted. Verified:\n\n```\nenqueue({delay:1000, priority:9})   // available at t+1000\nenqueue({delay:5000, priority:1})   // available at t+5000\n// at t+5000 -> dequeue returns the priority-1 task\n// spec wording requires the earlier-available (priority 9) task\n```\n\nThe phase-04 test only covers the case where both delayed tasks share the default priority, so the inversion is invisible to the suite. Note this genuinely conflicts with phase-03 (*\"dequeue() returns the highest priority task first\"*) — for **already-available** tasks priority must dominate, otherwise creation-time jitter in `availableAt` would break priority ordering.\n\nRecommended fix: make `availableAt` a tiebreaker that only applies among tasks that were *delayed*, or better, normalize the key so immediately-available tasks compare equal. Concretely, bucket by availability and order within the bucket by priority:\n\n```ts\nprivate comparePending(a: Task, b: Task, now: number): number {\n  // Tasks already available compare as one group (priority dominates);\n  // among delayed tasks, earlier availability wins first (phase-04).\n  const ad = a.availableAt.getTime() > now ? a.availableAt.getTime() : 0;\n  const bd = b.availableAt.getTime() > now ? b.availableAt.getTime() : 0;\n  if (ad !== bd) return ad - bd;\n  if (a.priority !== b.priority) return a.priority - b.priority;\n  return a.seq - b.seq;\n}\n```\n\nSince `dequeue` filters to available tasks first, `ad`/`bd` are both `0` in practice and priority governs — which satisfies phase-03, phase-08 (\"highest priority still chosen\"), and the phase-04 delayed-ordering test. If instead the intent is literal phase-04 compliance, please confirm which reading is authoritative before changing, because the two phases conflict and this is exactly the kind of deviation worth an explicit decision record in the plan.\n\n### H2. `fail()` on a task that was never dequeued grants unlimited retries (never reaches the DLQ)\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:327` and `:338-347`.\n\n`fail()` accepts `status === 'pending'`, but retry budget is derived from `attempts`, which only increments in `dequeue()` (`:279`). A task failed while pending has `attempts === 0`, so `retriesUsed = Math.max(0, 0 - 1) = 0`, which is always `< maxRetries` for any `maxRetries >= 1`. Verified:\n\n```\nenqueue('w', {}, { maxRetries: 1 });\nfor (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) queue.fail(t.id, 'e');\n// -> status 'pending', failCount 50, attempts 0, DLQ length 0\n```\n\nThe task loops forever and never reaches the DLQ, violating phase-06 (*\"When a task exhausts all retry attempts, it automatically moves to the DLQ\"*). Each such fail also resets `availableAt` to `now + backoffMs`, so a caller polling `fail()` can starve the task indefinitely.\n\nRecommended fix: gate retry accounting on `failCount` rather than `attempts`, which counts every failure regardless of dequeue state, and reject failing a non-processing task:\n\n```ts\nif (task.status !== 'processing') return task;   // only processing tasks can fail\n...\ntask.failCount += 1;\nconst retriesUsed = task.failCount - 1;          // 1st failure = 0 retries used\nif (retriesUsed < task.maxRetries) {\n  const backoff = task.backoffMs * Math.pow(2, retriesUsed);\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nUsing `failCount` also makes the backoff exponent independent of `attempts`, which matters for H3.\n\n### H3. `retryDLQ` resets `attempts` but not `failCount`, so the DLQ round is not actually \"fresh\" in reported state, and `failCount` is wrong per phase-06\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:369-382`.\n\nphase-06 requires both *\"DLQ tasks track how many times they've failed (`task.failCount`)\"* and *\"`retryDLQ(taskId)` resets the task's retry count, giving it a fresh set of retries.\"* The implementation resets `attempts = 0` (giving fresh retries — correct) but leaves `failCount`, `lastError`, `completedAt`, and stale progress intact. Verified: after exhausting `maxRetries: 2` then `retryDLQ`, `failCount` stays at `3` while `attempts` is back to `0` — the two counters now describe different lifetimes and `failCount` no longer means \"failures in the current round.\"\n\nThis is arguably intentional (cumulative failure history is useful), but combined with the H2 fix it becomes load-bearing: if retry budget is computed from `failCount`, then not resetting it means `retryDLQ` grants **zero** fresh retries and the task lands straight back in the DLQ on its first failure. Whichever counter drives the budget must be the one `retryDLQ` resets.\n\nRecommended fix: keep a cumulative `failCount` for reporting and track the budget separately, e.g. add a private `retriesUsed` field reset by `retryDLQ`:\n\n```ts\nretryDLQ(taskId: string): Task {\n  ...\n  task.status = 'pending';\n  task.attempts = 0;\n  task.retriesUsed = 0;          // fresh budget (phase-06)\n  task.availableAt = new Date(); // failCount/lastError retained as history\n  delete task.completedAt;\n  this.progress.delete(task.id); // stale 90% progress from the failed round\n  queue.pending.push(task);\n  return task;\n}\n```\n\nAlso: `retryDLQ` bypasses the shutdown guard. Verified it succeeds and re-populates a queue *after* `shutdown()` has resolved, resurrecting work in a dead queue. Add the `this.shuttingDown` check that `enqueue` has at `:189`.\n\n### H4. Permanent concurrency slot leak when `complete`/`fail`/`cancel` short-circuits on an already-settled status\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:307`, `:327`, `:505`.\n\nAll three methods return early *before* `queue.processing.delete(task.id)` when the status is not `pending`/`processing`. The `processing` set is therefore never cleaned for that id. Verified — a task whose status was changed by other means (phase-11 explicitly permits middleware to *\"modify the task object\"*) leaks a slot forever:\n\n```\nsetConcurrency('w', 1); enqueue; const t = dequeue('w');\nt.status = 'completed';       // middleware mutates the task\ncomplete(t.id);               // early-returns at :307, no processing.delete\ngetConcurrency('w')  ->  { current: 1, max: 1 }   // leaked permanently\ndequeue('w')         ->  null forever\n```\n\nThe same leak occurs via H5 (mutated `queueName`). Because `dequeue` gates on `processing.size >= concurrency` (`:260`), one leak permanently reduces throughput and enough leaks deadlock the queue.\n\nRecommended fix: release the slot unconditionally before the status guard, since removing a stale id is idempotent and harmless:\n\n```ts\ncomplete(taskId: string): Task | null {\n  const task = this.tasks.get(taskId);\n  if (!task) throw new Error(`Unknown task: ${taskId}`);\n  const queue = this.queues.get(task.queueName);\n  queue?.processing.delete(task.id);        // always release, even if already settled\n  if (task.status !== 'processing' && task.status !== 'pending') return task;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nApply the same reordering in `fail()` and `cancel()`.\n\n### H5. Slot released from the wrong queue when a task's `queueName` is mutated\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:309-311`, `:329-331`, `:507-509` — all use `this.getOrCreateQueue(task.queueName)`.\n\n`dequeue` adds the id to the queue it was dequeued from, but `complete`/`fail`/`cancel` look up the queue by the task's *current* `queueName`. Since middleware may legally mutate the task, these can diverge. Verified: after mutating `queueName` from `'w'` to `'other'`, `complete()` deletes from `'other'`'s empty set, leaving `'w'` at `current: 1, max: 1` forever, and it silently creates a spurious `'other'` queue that shows up in `listQueues()`.\n\nRecommended fix: record the owning queue at dequeue time on a private field (e.g. `task.processingQueue = queueName`) and use that for slot release; or search all queues' `processing` sets for the id. Also prefer `this.queues.get(...)` over `getOrCreateQueue(...)` in these three settle paths so a bad `queueName` cannot conjure a queue as a side effect of completing a task.\n\n---\n\n## MEDIUM\n\n### M1. Middleware that skips `next()` marks the task **completed**\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:604-608`.\n\nphase-11 says *\"If a middleware does NOT call `next()`, the task processing is skipped.\"* The code cannot distinguish \"handler ran and left the task processing\" from \"pipeline short-circuited,\" so it unconditionally calls `complete()`. Verified: a middleware that returns without calling `next()` yields `status: 'completed'` — the task is reported as successfully done despite never being handled. This is the classic auth/rate-limit middleware pattern (skip the task, leave it for later), and silently completing it means permanent data loss. The phase-11 test only asserts the handler didn't run, so the wrong terminal status is not caught.\n\nRecommended fix: track whether the handler actually executed and only auto-complete in that case:\n\n```ts\nlet handlerRan = false;\nconst dispatch = async (index: number): Promise<void> => {\n  ...\n  if (index === middleware.length) { handlerRan = true; await handler(task); return; }\n  ...\n};\ntry { await dispatch(0); } catch (error) { this.fail(task.id, error); return task; }\n\nif (task.status === 'processing') {\n  if (handlerRan) this.complete(task.id);\n  else this.releaseSkipped(task);   // return to pending, restore the slot\n}\n```\n\nWhere `releaseSkipped` sets `status = 'pending'`, decrements `attempts`, removes the id from `processing`, and pushes back to `pending`. Please confirm the intended semantics for a skipped task (re-queued vs. dropped vs. left processing) and record it in the plan — the spec is ambiguous, but \"silently completed\" is the one reading that is clearly wrong.\n\n### M2. `next()` called twice fails a task whose handler already succeeded\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:584-588`, `:599-601`.\n\nThe double-`next()` guard throws, and the `catch` at `:599` routes that into `this.fail(task.id, error)`. Verified: with `async (_t, next) => { await next(); await next(); }`, the handler runs exactly once and succeeds, yet the task ends `status: 'failed'` and is sent to the DLQ. A middleware authoring bug thus corrupts a successfully-processed task's outcome, and (worse) triggers a retry that re-executes a handler which already had its side effects.\n\nRecommended fix: distinguish pipeline-contract violations from task errors. Either don't throw at all (make the second `next()` a no-op with a warning), or re-throw the guard error out of `process()` instead of feeding it to `fail()`:\n\n```ts\nclass MiddlewareContractError extends Error {}\n...\ntry { await dispatch(0); }\ncatch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof MiddlewareContractError) throw error;  // programmer error, not task failure\n  this.fail(task.id, error);\n  return task;\n}\n```\n\n### M3. `tick()` throws mid-loop during shutdown, leaving schedules partially advanced\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:552-559`.\n\n`tick()` mutates `entry.nextRunAt` at `:557` and *then* calls `this.enqueue(...)` at `:558`, which throws once `shuttingDown` is set. Verified with two due schedules:\n\n```\na.nextRunAt -> advanced to 00:02   (advanced, but no task enqueued: occurrence silently lost)\nb.nextRunAt -> still 00:01         (loop aborted before reaching it)\n```\n\nSo `tick()` propagates an exception to a caller that has no reason to expect one, drops one occurrence, and leaves the schedule set in an inconsistent state. The same partial-advance corruption occurs for any `enqueue` throw (e.g. a schedule created with a `taskTemplate` that later hits a validation error).\n\nRecommended fix: guard and make the loop failure-atomic per entry:\n\n```ts\ntick(): Task[] {\n  if (this.shuttingDown) return [];\n  const now = Date.now();\n  const created: Task[] = [];\n  for (const entry of this.schedules.values()) {\n    if (!entry.active || entry.nextRunAt.getTime() > now) continue;\n    const next = nextOccurrence(parseCron(entry.cronExpression), now);\n    try {\n      created.push(this.enqueue(entry.queueName, clonePayload(entry.taskTemplate)));\n      entry.nextRunAt = next;      // only advance on successful enqueue\n    } catch { /* leave nextRunAt so the occurrence is retried next tick */ }\n  }\n  return created;\n}\n```\n\nAlso cache the parsed `CronSpec` on the schedule instead of re-parsing on every tick (`:556`) — it's re-validated on every call for no benefit.\n\n### M4. Shutdown `completed` counts tasks that were never in flight\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:307`, `:316-319`.\n\n`complete()` accepts `status === 'pending'`, and the shutdown counter increments for any completion while `shutdownState` is set. phase-12 defines `completed` as *\"number of tasks that completed during shutdown,\"* which in context means the in-flight tasks shutdown waited for. Verified: with 1 in-flight task and 5 untouched pending tasks, completing all of them reports `completed: 6`. The summary therefore over-reports and can't be used to reconcile against `cancelled`.\n\nRecommended fix: only count completions of tasks that were actually processing when shutdown began. Snapshot the in-flight id set at `shutdown()` time and increment only for members of it:\n\n```ts\nconst inFlight = new Set<string>();\nfor (const q of this.queues.values()) for (const id of q.processing) inFlight.add(id);\nstate.inFlight = inFlight;\n...\n// in complete():\nif (this.shutdownState?.inFlight.has(task.id)) { this.shutdownState.completed += 1; }\n```\n\nThis also fixes the related double-count: `complete(id)` twice on the same task currently reports `completed: 2` only because the first call left it settled — with a snapshot set you can `delete` the id on first settle and make it exactly-once.\n\n### M5. A task that fails during shutdown is silently retried and never accounted for\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:349-351`.\n\n`fail()` during shutdown re-enqueues the task with a backoff (`:339-343`), then calls `maybeFinishShutdown()`. Since `countProcessing()` is now 0, shutdown resolves. Verified:\n\n```\nshutdown(10000); fail(t.id, 'boom');\n-> summary { completed: 0, cancelled: 0, elapsed: 0 }, task status 'pending', queueSize 1\n```\n\nThe task is neither `completed` nor `cancelled` in the summary — it's left `pending` in a queue that will never be drained, so the work is lost with no record. phase-12 says force-cancelled tasks get `status: 'cancelled'`; a task failing during shutdown should not quietly resurrect as pending.\n\nRecommended fix: suppress retry once shutting down and count it:\n\n```ts\n// in fail(), before the retry branch:\nif (this.shuttingDown && retriesUsed < task.maxRetries) {\n  task.status = 'cancelled';\n  this.shutdownState!.cancelled += 1;\n  this.maybeFinishShutdown();\n  return task;\n}\n```\n\nSame consideration applies to `cancel()` at `:512-514`: a manual `cancel()` during shutdown fires `maybeFinishShutdown()` but never increments `state.cancelled` (verified: `{ completed: 0, cancelled: 0 }` after cancelling the only in-flight task), so the summary under-reports there too.\n\n### M6. Async `onShutdown` callbacks produce unhandled promise rejections\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:642-648`.\n\n`void callback()` inside `try/catch` only catches *synchronous* throws. An `async` callback that rejects escapes as an unhandled rejection — verified, vitest reports `Unhandled Rejection: Error: async hook boom`, which in Node with default settings crashes the process on `unhandledRejection`. Ironic given the comment *\"Shutdown hooks must never block shutdown.\"* Additionally, async hooks are not awaited at all, so shutdown can resolve before cleanup hooks finish.\n\nRecommended fix:\n\n```ts\nconst hookResults = this.shutdownCallbacks.map((cb) => {\n  try { return Promise.resolve(cb()).catch(() => undefined); }\n  catch { return Promise.resolve(); }\n});\n// optionally: await Promise.all(hookResults) before resolving, so hooks complete\n```\n\n### M7. Non-integer / NaN concurrency limits are accepted and mis-enforce\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:444-451`.\n\nThe validator rejects only `NaN` and negatives. `setConcurrency('w', 2.7)` is stored verbatim, and because the gate is `processing.size >= concurrency`, `2 >= 2.7` is false — so **three** tasks dequeue against a limit of 2.7. Verified: `{ current: 3, max: 2.7 }`. `getConcurrency` also surfaces the fractional `max` to callers.\n\nRecommended fix: require a non-negative integer (or floor it), and reject `Infinity` explicitly or document it as equivalent to 0:\n\n```ts\nif (typeof limit !== 'number' || !Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 0) {\n  throw new Error(`Invalid concurrency limit: ${String(limit)}`);\n}\n```\n\n### M8. `shutdown(NaN)` force-cancels immediately instead of using a sane timeout\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:656`.\n\n`Math.max(0, NaN)` is `NaN`, and `setTimeout(fn, NaN)` is coerced to `0`. Verified: `shutdown(NaN)` force-cancels the in-flight task on the next tick (`{ cancelled: 1 }`) rather than waiting. A caller passing a computed timeout that turns out `NaN` (e.g. `parseInt` of a missing env var) gets an immediate hard kill of all in-flight work — the opposite of graceful.\n\nRecommended fix: `const ms = Number.isFinite(timeout) && timeout >= 0 ? timeout : DEFAULT_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS;`\n\n---\n\n## LOW\n\n### L1. Cycle detection recurses without depth limit — stack overflow on deep chains\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:425-437`.\n\n`visit` is recursive. Verified: a 20 000-deep dependency chain throws `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` from `enqueue`. It's also O(chain length) per enqueue with a fresh `visited` set each call, making chain construction O(n²) — building that chain took 5.7s. Recommended fix: convert to an explicit stack/iterative DFS, and consider caching a transitive-closure or depth per task so each enqueue is O(direct deps).\n\n### L2. `getDependencies` fabricates `'failed'` for unknown dependency ids\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:397-400`.\n\n`this.tasks.get(depId)?.status ?? ('failed' as TaskStatus)` reports a missing task as `failed`, conflating \"this dependency failed\" with \"this dependency does not exist.\" `enqueue` validates existence at `:417`, so this is only reachable if a task is evicted later, but the `as TaskStatus` cast is a deliberate hole in the type system for a value the spec never defines. Recommended fix: widen `DependencyInfo['status']` to include `'unknown'` (or make it `TaskStatus | 'missing'`) and report that, rather than lying with `'failed'` — a dependent blocked on a nonexistent id is a very different diagnosis.\n\n### L3. `cancel()` cannot remove a task from the DLQ\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:502-516`.\n\n`cancel()` guards on `pending`/`processing`, so a DLQ task (status `failed`) is a no-op — verified `status` stays `failed` and DLQ length stays 1. `clearDLQ` is all-or-nothing per queue, so there is no way to discard a single poison-pill DLQ entry. Recommended fix: either let `cancel()` splice the task out of `queue.dlq` and mark it `cancelled`, or add `removeFromDLQ(taskId)`.\n\n### L4. `complete()` on a DLQ/failed task is a no-op, but a failed dependency can never unblock dependents\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:307` combined with `:403-409`.\n\nphase-07 correctly says a failed dependency leaves dependents blocked, and that is the behavior (verified: dependent stays `pending`, `dequeue` returns `null`). But since `complete()` refuses to act on a `failed` task and `retryDLQ` doesn't help until the retried task actually completes, a dependent whose dependency dies is blocked forever with no API to resolve it and no signal to the operator. Recommended fix: out of scope for the 12 phases, but worth a plan note — either expose `forceComplete(taskId)` or surface blocked-forever tasks (e.g. a `getBlocked()` helper) so operators can detect the condition. Also, `dependsOn` is never garbage-collected from the `tasks` map, so the map grows without bound for long-lived queues.\n\n### L5. `clonePayload` silently degrades non-plain-object templates\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:558`, `:760-768`.\n\nphase-10 says *\"The `taskTemplate` is used as the payload.\"* The hand-rolled deep clone converts `Date` → plain object, `Map`/`Set` → `{}`, and drops prototypes; verified `payload.d instanceof Date === false` and `payload.nested instanceof Map === false` for a template containing them. Functions survive by reference (they hit the `typeof !== 'object'` branch), which is inconsistent. It also has no cycle detection, so a self-referential template infinite-loops. Recommended fix: use `structuredClone` (Node 17+, and `randomUUID` already assumes modern Node) with a fallback, and note that `structuredClone` throws on functions — which is arguably the correct signal for a task payload.\n\n### L6. Hourly cron skips the repeated hour during DST fall-back\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:747-751`.\n\n`next.setMinutes(0,0,0)` then `next.setHours(date.getHours() + 1)` operates on local wall-clock hours, so during the ambiguous hour the repeated local hour is skipped. Verified in `America/New_York`: from `01:30 EDT` the next fire is `02:00 EST` (90 minutes later), skipping the second `01:00 EST`. Real cron fires both. Daily and `*/N` are correct across both transitions. Low priority since the spec's cron subset is minimal and the tests run in UTC, but worth a comment documenting the local-time semantics. Recommended fix: compute hourly boundaries by UTC epoch arithmetic (`Math.floor(from / HOUR_MS) * HOUR_MS + HOUR_MS`) if UTC semantics are acceptable, matching how the `minutes` branch already works at `:739`.\n\n### L7. Minor spec/API inconsistencies\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:201-204` — `maxRetries: 2.5` is accepted verbatim; `Number.isInteger` check would match the priority validation rigor. `backoffMs: Infinity` also passes and yields an `Invalid Date` for `availableAt`.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:340` — `backoffMs * 2^attempts` overflows to `Infinity` around attempt ~1070, producing `availableAt` = `Invalid Date`; comparisons against `NaN` are always false so the task becomes permanently un-dequeuable. Clamp: `Math.min(backoff, MAX_BACKOFF_MS)`.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:461-481` — `updateProgress` accepts `pending`, `cancelled`, and `completed` tasks (verified progress recorded at 100% on a cancelled task); phase-09 says *\"Report progress for a processing task.\"*\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:474-479` — a throwing `onProgress` listener aborts the loop, so later subscribers never fire and the exception propagates to the `updateProgress` caller. Verified: with listeners `[a, b]`, only `a` runs and `updateProgress` throws. Wrap each listener in `try/catch`.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:180-187` — `enqueue('some-string')` treats the single string argument as a *queue name* with `payload: undefined`, silently creating a queue. Ambiguity is inherent to the overload, but phase-01/02 back-compat (`enqueue(payload)`) means a string payload is unreachable. Worth a documented note.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:335` — `task.lastError = error instanceof Error ? error.message : (error as string)` casts an arbitrary value to `string`; verified `fail(id, { code: 500 })` stores an *object* in a field typed `string | undefined`. Use `String(error)` for the non-Error branch.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:266-277` — `dequeue` is O(pending) per call with an O(n) `splice`, so draining a queue is O(n²). Fine at test scale; a priority heap or per-priority buckets would be needed for large queues.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended fix order\n\n1. **H4 + H5** — permanent slot leaks that deadlock queues; smallest, safest diffs.\n2. **H2 + H3** — retry/DLQ accounting; fix together since the budget counter choice couples them.\n3. **M1 + M2** — middleware pipeline terminal states (silent completion is data loss).\n4. **H1** — needs a plan decision on the phase-03/phase-04 conflict before coding.\n5. **M3–M8**, then LOW.\n\nAll 90 phase tests pass before and after my inspection; I edited nothing. Note that H1's fix as written above is deliberately behavior-preserving for the existing suite — if you adopt the literal phase-04 reading instead, `tests/phase-08.test.ts` (\"concurrency respects priority ordering\") and `tests/phase-03.test.ts` will need review, which is itself evidence the spec wording should be amended rather than the comparator."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a6f7bc158d1a3ff3a (use SendMessage with to: 'a6f7bc158d1a3ff3a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 89004\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 422524</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbaa2cb-485d-41ba-ae15-3484acaf3773","uuid":"68504fdd-9b94-4a18-9140-828174c08416","timestamp":"2026-07-25T01:35:14.555Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Review /workspace/src/index.ts (a TaskQueue implementation, the only source file; committed in HEAD, base commit 2c51bef). The spec is in /workspace/phases/phase-01.md through phase-12.md and /workspace/TASK.md. Tests are in /workspace/tests/ (all 90 currently pass; build and lint are clean — do not break them).\n\nFocus on CORRECTNESS and EDGE CASES beyond what the tests cover, and on spec compliance for each of the 12 phases. Specifically scrutinize:\n- dequeue ordering (priority vs availableAt vs FIFO) versus the phase-04 spec wording\n- retry/backoff math and maxRetries accounting\n- DLQ semantics (failCount, retryDLQ freshness)\n- dependency cycle detection completeness and getDependencies for missing tasks\n- concurrency accounting leaks (does any path forget to release a processing slot?)\n- cancel/progress semantics\n- cron next-occurrence math (DST/rollover, `0 * * * *`, `0 0 * * *`)\n- middleware pipeline error handling, and what happens when a middleware skips next()\n- shutdown accounting (completed/cancelled counts, idempotency, timers left dangling, tasks failed during shutdown)\n\nReport findings classified HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW priority with file:line references and a concrete recommended fix for each. Do not edit any files. Be precise and skeptical; call out real bugs, not style nits.","agentId":"a6f7bc158d1a3ff3a","agentType":"conclave:code-reviewer","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 90 spec tests pass. The `tests/tmp-adv*.test.ts` files are untracked artifacts created during this session's timeframe by another agent (not by me — I only used `/workspace/.probe/`, now deleted). They contain 12 failing assertions; worth flagging to the parent agent since `npm test` currently reports failures because of them.\n\nHere are my findings.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe implementation is well-structured: a single source of truth (`tasks` map), per-queue state objects, a comparator-based `dequeue`, and a `seq` counter for stable FIFO. Overload handling in `enqueue`, the `dependenciesSatisfied` check folded into `isAvailable`, and clearing the shutdown timer on early resolve are all done correctly. Cron DST rollover via `setHours`/`setDate` is genuinely correct (verified under `TZ=America/New_York` across both spring-forward and fall-back), and the retry backoff sequence on the normal dequeue→fail path is exact (`100, 200, 400, 800, 1600` for `backoffMs: 100`).\n\nThat said, there are several real correctness bugs, two of which are unbounded/permanent state corruption.\n\n**Environment note first:** `/workspace/tests/tmp-adv2.test.ts`, `tmp-adv3.test.ts`, `tmp-adversarial.test.ts` are untracked leftovers (not mine) that make `npm test` report **12 failed / 237 total**. The 12 real phase suites pass 90/90. These stray files should be deleted before anyone reads `npm test` output as a signal.\n\n---\n\n## HIGH\n\n### H1. `dequeue` ordering contradicts the phase-04 spec (priority and availability precedence are swapped)\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:294-300` (`comparePending`), used at `:269`.\n\nphase-04.md states: *\"When multiple delayed tasks are available, they are ordered by their availability time (earliest first), then by priority, then FIFO.\"* The comparator sorts priority first, then `availableAt`, then `seq` — the documented first two keys are inverted. Verified:\n\n```\nenqueue({delay:1000, priority:9})   // available at t+1000\nenqueue({delay:5000, priority:1})   // available at t+5000\n// at t+5000 -> dequeue returns the priority-1 task\n// spec wording requires the earlier-available (priority 9) task\n```\n\nThe phase-04 test only covers the case where both delayed tasks share the default priority, so the inversion is invisible to the suite. Note this genuinely conflicts with phase-03 (*\"dequeue() returns the highest priority task first\"*) — for **already-available** tasks priority must dominate, otherwise creation-time jitter in `availableAt` would break priority ordering.\n\nRecommended fix: make `availableAt` a tiebreaker that only applies among tasks that were *delayed*, or better, normalize the key so immediately-available tasks compare equal. Concretely, bucket by availability and order within the bucket by priority:\n\n```ts\nprivate comparePending(a: Task, b: Task, now: number): number {\n  // Tasks already available compare as one group (priority dominates);\n  // among delayed tasks, earlier availability wins first (phase-04).\n  const ad = a.availableAt.getTime() > now ? a.availableAt.getTime() : 0;\n  const bd = b.availableAt.getTime() > now ? b.availableAt.getTime() : 0;\n  if (ad !== bd) return ad - bd;\n  if (a.priority !== b.priority) return a.priority - b.priority;\n  return a.seq - b.seq;\n}\n```\n\nSince `dequeue` filters to available tasks first, `ad`/`bd` are both `0` in practice and priority governs — which satisfies phase-03, phase-08 (\"highest priority still chosen\"), and the phase-04 delayed-ordering test. If instead the intent is literal phase-04 compliance, please confirm which reading is authoritative before changing, because the two phases conflict and this is exactly the kind of deviation worth an explicit decision record in the plan.\n\n### H2. `fail()` on a task that was never dequeued grants unlimited retries (never reaches the DLQ)\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:327` and `:338-347`.\n\n`fail()` accepts `status === 'pending'`, but retry budget is derived from `attempts`, which only increments in `dequeue()` (`:279`). A task failed while pending has `attempts === 0`, so `retriesUsed = Math.max(0, 0 - 1) = 0`, which is always `< maxRetries` for any `maxRetries >= 1`. Verified:\n\n```\nenqueue('w', {}, { maxRetries: 1 });\nfor (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) queue.fail(t.id, 'e');\n// -> status 'pending', failCount 50, attempts 0, DLQ length 0\n```\n\nThe task loops forever and never reaches the DLQ, violating phase-06 (*\"When a task exhausts all retry attempts, it automatically moves to the DLQ\"*). Each such fail also resets `availableAt` to `now + backoffMs`, so a caller polling `fail()` can starve the task indefinitely.\n\nRecommended fix: gate retry accounting on `failCount` rather than `attempts`, which counts every failure regardless of dequeue state, and reject failing a non-processing task:\n\n```ts\nif (task.status !== 'processing') return task;   // only processing tasks can fail\n...\ntask.failCount += 1;\nconst retriesUsed = task.failCount - 1;          // 1st failure = 0 retries used\nif (retriesUsed < task.maxRetries) {\n  const backoff = task.backoffMs * Math.pow(2, retriesUsed);\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nUsing `failCount` also makes the backoff exponent independent of `attempts`, which matters for H3.\n\n### H3. `retryDLQ` resets `attempts` but not `failCount`, so the DLQ round is not actually \"fresh\" in reported state, and `failCount` is wrong per phase-06\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:369-382`.\n\nphase-06 requires both *\"DLQ tasks track how many times they've failed (`task.failCount`)\"* and *\"`retryDLQ(taskId)` resets the task's retry count, giving it a fresh set of retries.\"* The implementation resets `attempts = 0` (giving fresh retries — correct) but leaves `failCount`, `lastError`, `completedAt`, and stale progress intact. Verified: after exhausting `maxRetries: 2` then `retryDLQ`, `failCount` stays at `3` while `attempts` is back to `0` — the two counters now describe different lifetimes and `failCount` no longer means \"failures in the current round.\"\n\nThis is arguably intentional (cumulative failure history is useful), but combined with the H2 fix it becomes load-bearing: if retry budget is computed from `failCount`, then not resetting it means `retryDLQ` grants **zero** fresh retries and the task lands straight back in the DLQ on its first failure. Whichever counter drives the budget must be the one `retryDLQ` resets.\n\nRecommended fix: keep a cumulative `failCount` for reporting and track the budget separately, e.g. add a private `retriesUsed` field reset by `retryDLQ`:\n\n```ts\nretryDLQ(taskId: string): Task {\n  ...\n  task.status = 'pending';\n  task.attempts = 0;\n  task.retriesUsed = 0;          // fresh budget (phase-06)\n  task.availableAt = new Date(); // failCount/lastError retained as history\n  delete task.completedAt;\n  this.progress.delete(task.id); // stale 90% progress from the failed round\n  queue.pending.push(task);\n  return task;\n}\n```\n\nAlso: `retryDLQ` bypasses the shutdown guard. Verified it succeeds and re-populates a queue *after* `shutdown()` has resolved, resurrecting work in a dead queue. Add the `this.shuttingDown` check that `enqueue` has at `:189`.\n\n### H4. Permanent concurrency slot leak when `complete`/`fail`/`cancel` short-circuits on an already-settled status\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:307`, `:327`, `:505`.\n\nAll three methods return early *before* `queue.processing.delete(task.id)` when the status is not `pending`/`processing`. The `processing` set is therefore never cleaned for that id. Verified — a task whose status was changed by other means (phase-11 explicitly permits middleware to *\"modify the task object\"*) leaks a slot forever:\n\n```\nsetConcurrency('w', 1); enqueue; const t = dequeue('w');\nt.status = 'completed';       // middleware mutates the task\ncomplete(t.id);               // early-returns at :307, no processing.delete\ngetConcurrency('w')  ->  { current: 1, max: 1 }   // leaked permanently\ndequeue('w')         ->  null forever\n```\n\nThe same leak occurs via H5 (mutated `queueName`). Because `dequeue` gates on `processing.size >= concurrency` (`:260`), one leak permanently reduces throughput and enough leaks deadlock the queue.\n\nRecommended fix: release the slot unconditionally before the status guard, since removing a stale id is idempotent and harmless:\n\n```ts\ncomplete(taskId: string): Task | null {\n  const task = this.tasks.get(taskId);\n  if (!task) throw new Error(`Unknown task: ${taskId}`);\n  const queue = this.queues.get(task.queueName);\n  queue?.processing.delete(task.id);        // always release, even if already settled\n  if (task.status !== 'processing' && task.status !== 'pending') return task;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nApply the same reordering in `fail()` and `cancel()`.\n\n### H5. Slot released from the wrong queue when a task's `queueName` is mutated\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:309-311`, `:329-331`, `:507-509` — all use `this.getOrCreateQueue(task.queueName)`.\n\n`dequeue` adds the id to the queue it was dequeued from, but `complete`/`fail`/`cancel` look up the queue by the task's *current* `queueName`. Since middleware may legally mutate the task, these can diverge. Verified: after mutating `queueName` from `'w'` to `'other'`, `complete()` deletes from `'other'`'s empty set, leaving `'w'` at `current: 1, max: 1` forever, and it silently creates a spurious `'other'` queue that shows up in `listQueues()`.\n\nRecommended fix: record the owning queue at dequeue time on a private field (e.g. `task.processingQueue = queueName`) and use that for slot release; or search all queues' `processing` sets for the id. Also prefer `this.queues.get(...)` over `getOrCreateQueue(...)` in these three settle paths so a bad `queueName` cannot conjure a queue as a side effect of completing a task.\n\n---\n\n## MEDIUM\n\n### M1. Middleware that skips `next()` marks the task **completed**\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:604-608`.\n\nphase-11 says *\"If a middleware does NOT call `next()`, the task processing is skipped.\"* The code cannot distinguish \"handler ran and left the task processing\" from \"pipeline short-circuited,\" so it unconditionally calls `complete()`. Verified: a middleware that returns without calling `next()` yields `status: 'completed'` — the task is reported as successfully done despite never being handled. This is the classic auth/rate-limit middleware pattern (skip the task, leave it for later), and silently completing it means permanent data loss. The phase-11 test only asserts the handler didn't run, so the wrong terminal status is not caught.\n\nRecommended fix: track whether the handler actually executed and only auto-complete in that case:\n\n```ts\nlet handlerRan = false;\nconst dispatch = async (index: number): Promise<void> => {\n  ...\n  if (index === middleware.length) { handlerRan = true; await handler(task); return; }\n  ...\n};\ntry { await dispatch(0); } catch (error) { this.fail(task.id, error); return task; }\n\nif (task.status === 'processing') {\n  if (handlerRan) this.complete(task.id);\n  else this.releaseSkipped(task);   // return to pending, restore the slot\n}\n```\n\nWhere `releaseSkipped` sets `status = 'pending'`, decrements `attempts`, removes the id from `processing`, and pushes back to `pending`. Please confirm the intended semantics for a skipped task (re-queued vs. dropped vs. left processing) and record it in the plan — the spec is ambiguous, but \"silently completed\" is the one reading that is clearly wrong.\n\n### M2. `next()` called twice fails a task whose handler already succeeded\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:584-588`, `:599-601`.\n\nThe double-`next()` guard throws, and the `catch` at `:599` routes that into `this.fail(task.id, error)`. Verified: with `async (_t, next) => { await next(); await next(); }`, the handler runs exactly once and succeeds, yet the task ends `status: 'failed'` and is sent to the DLQ. A middleware authoring bug thus corrupts a successfully-processed task's outcome, and (worse) triggers a retry that re-executes a handler which already had its side effects.\n\nRecommended fix: distinguish pipeline-contract violations from task errors. Either don't throw at all (make the second `next()` a no-op with a warning), or re-throw the guard error out of `process()` instead of feeding it to `fail()`:\n\n```ts\nclass MiddlewareContractError extends Error {}\n...\ntry { await dispatch(0); }\ncatch (error) {\n  if (error instanceof MiddlewareContractError) throw error;  // programmer error, not task failure\n  this.fail(task.id, error);\n  return task;\n}\n```\n\n### M3. `tick()` throws mid-loop during shutdown, leaving schedules partially advanced\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:552-559`.\n\n`tick()` mutates `entry.nextRunAt` at `:557` and *then* calls `this.enqueue(...)` at `:558`, which throws once `shuttingDown` is set. Verified with two due schedules:\n\n```\na.nextRunAt -> advanced to 00:02   (advanced, but no task enqueued: occurrence silently lost)\nb.nextRunAt -> still 00:01         (loop aborted before reaching it)\n```\n\nSo `tick()` propagates an exception to a caller that has no reason to expect one, drops one occurrence, and leaves the schedule set in an inconsistent state. The same partial-advance corruption occurs for any `enqueue` throw (e.g. a schedule created with a `taskTemplate` that later hits a validation error).\n\nRecommended fix: guard and make the loop failure-atomic per entry:\n\n```ts\ntick(): Task[] {\n  if (this.shuttingDown) return [];\n  const now = Date.now();\n  const created: Task[] = [];\n  for (const entry of this.schedules.values()) {\n    if (!entry.active || entry.nextRunAt.getTime() > now) continue;\n    const next = nextOccurrence(parseCron(entry.cronExpression), now);\n    try {\n      created.push(this.enqueue(entry.queueName, clonePayload(entry.taskTemplate)));\n      entry.nextRunAt = next;      // only advance on successful enqueue\n    } catch { /* leave nextRunAt so the occurrence is retried next tick */ }\n  }\n  return created;\n}\n```\n\nAlso cache the parsed `CronSpec` on the schedule instead of re-parsing on every tick (`:556`) — it's re-validated on every call for no benefit.\n\n### M4. Shutdown `completed` counts tasks that were never in flight\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:307`, `:316-319`.\n\n`complete()` accepts `status === 'pending'`, and the shutdown counter increments for any completion while `shutdownState` is set. phase-12 defines `completed` as *\"number of tasks that completed during shutdown,\"* which in context means the in-flight tasks shutdown waited for. Verified: with 1 in-flight task and 5 untouched pending tasks, completing all of them reports `completed: 6`. The summary therefore over-reports and can't be used to reconcile against `cancelled`.\n\nRecommended fix: only count completions of tasks that were actually processing when shutdown began. Snapshot the in-flight id set at `shutdown()` time and increment only for members of it:\n\n```ts\nconst inFlight = new Set<string>();\nfor (const q of this.queues.values()) for (const id of q.processing) inFlight.add(id);\nstate.inFlight = inFlight;\n...\n// in complete():\nif (this.shutdownState?.inFlight.has(task.id)) { this.shutdownState.completed += 1; }\n```\n\nThis also fixes the related double-count: `complete(id)` twice on the same task currently reports `completed: 2` only because the first call left it settled — with a snapshot set you can `delete` the id on first settle and make it exactly-once.\n\n### M5. A task that fails during shutdown is silently retried and never accounted for\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:349-351`.\n\n`fail()` during shutdown re-enqueues the task with a backoff (`:339-343`), then calls `maybeFinishShutdown()`. Since `countProcessing()` is now 0, shutdown resolves. Verified:\n\n```\nshutdown(10000); fail(t.id, 'boom');\n-> summary { completed: 0, cancelled: 0, elapsed: 0 }, task status 'pending', queueSize 1\n```\n\nThe task is neither `completed` nor `cancelled` in the summary — it's left `pending` in a queue that will never be drained, so the work is lost with no record. phase-12 says force-cancelled tasks get `status: 'cancelled'`; a task failing during shutdown should not quietly resurrect as pending.\n\nRecommended fix: suppress retry once shutting down and count it:\n\n```ts\n// in fail(), before the retry branch:\nif (this.shuttingDown && retriesUsed < task.maxRetries) {\n  task.status = 'cancelled';\n  this.shutdownState!.cancelled += 1;\n  this.maybeFinishShutdown();\n  return task;\n}\n```\n\nSame consideration applies to `cancel()` at `:512-514`: a manual `cancel()` during shutdown fires `maybeFinishShutdown()` but never increments `state.cancelled` (verified: `{ completed: 0, cancelled: 0 }` after cancelling the only in-flight task), so the summary under-reports there too.\n\n### M6. Async `onShutdown` callbacks produce unhandled promise rejections\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:642-648`.\n\n`void callback()` inside `try/catch` only catches *synchronous* throws. An `async` callback that rejects escapes as an unhandled rejection — verified, vitest reports `Unhandled Rejection: Error: async hook boom`, which in Node with default settings crashes the process on `unhandledRejection`. Ironic given the comment *\"Shutdown hooks must never block shutdown.\"* Additionally, async hooks are not awaited at all, so shutdown can resolve before cleanup hooks finish.\n\nRecommended fix:\n\n```ts\nconst hookResults = this.shutdownCallbacks.map((cb) => {\n  try { return Promise.resolve(cb()).catch(() => undefined); }\n  catch { return Promise.resolve(); }\n});\n// optionally: await Promise.all(hookResults) before resolving, so hooks complete\n```\n\n### M7. Non-integer / NaN concurrency limits are accepted and mis-enforce\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:444-451`.\n\nThe validator rejects only `NaN` and negatives. `setConcurrency('w', 2.7)` is stored verbatim, and because the gate is `processing.size >= concurrency`, `2 >= 2.7` is false — so **three** tasks dequeue against a limit of 2.7. Verified: `{ current: 3, max: 2.7 }`. `getConcurrency` also surfaces the fractional `max` to callers.\n\nRecommended fix: require a non-negative integer (or floor it), and reject `Infinity` explicitly or document it as equivalent to 0:\n\n```ts\nif (typeof limit !== 'number' || !Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 0) {\n  throw new Error(`Invalid concurrency limit: ${String(limit)}`);\n}\n```\n\n### M8. `shutdown(NaN)` force-cancels immediately instead of using a sane timeout\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:656`.\n\n`Math.max(0, NaN)` is `NaN`, and `setTimeout(fn, NaN)` is coerced to `0`. Verified: `shutdown(NaN)` force-cancels the in-flight task on the next tick (`{ cancelled: 1 }`) rather than waiting. A caller passing a computed timeout that turns out `NaN` (e.g. `parseInt` of a missing env var) gets an immediate hard kill of all in-flight work — the opposite of graceful.\n\nRecommended fix: `const ms = Number.isFinite(timeout) && timeout >= 0 ? timeout : DEFAULT_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS;`\n\n---\n\n## LOW\n\n### L1. Cycle detection recurses without depth limit — stack overflow on deep chains\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:425-437`.\n\n`visit` is recursive. Verified: a 20 000-deep dependency chain throws `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` from `enqueue`. It's also O(chain length) per enqueue with a fresh `visited` set each call, making chain construction O(n²) — building that chain took 5.7s. Recommended fix: convert to an explicit stack/iterative DFS, and consider caching a transitive-closure or depth per task so each enqueue is O(direct deps).\n\n### L2. `getDependencies` fabricates `'failed'` for unknown dependency ids\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:397-400`.\n\n`this.tasks.get(depId)?.status ?? ('failed' as TaskStatus)` reports a missing task as `failed`, conflating \"this dependency failed\" with \"this dependency does not exist.\" `enqueue` validates existence at `:417`, so this is only reachable if a task is evicted later, but the `as TaskStatus` cast is a deliberate hole in the type system for a value the spec never defines. Recommended fix: widen `DependencyInfo['status']` to include `'unknown'` (or make it `TaskStatus | 'missing'`) and report that, rather than lying with `'failed'` — a dependent blocked on a nonexistent id is a very different diagnosis.\n\n### L3. `cancel()` cannot remove a task from the DLQ\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:502-516`.\n\n`cancel()` guards on `pending`/`processing`, so a DLQ task (status `failed`) is a no-op — verified `status` stays `failed` and DLQ length stays 1. `clearDLQ` is all-or-nothing per queue, so there is no way to discard a single poison-pill DLQ entry. Recommended fix: either let `cancel()` splice the task out of `queue.dlq` and mark it `cancelled`, or add `removeFromDLQ(taskId)`.\n\n### L4. `complete()` on a DLQ/failed task is a no-op, but a failed dependency can never unblock dependents\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:307` combined with `:403-409`.\n\nphase-07 correctly says a failed dependency leaves dependents blocked, and that is the behavior (verified: dependent stays `pending`, `dequeue` returns `null`). But since `complete()` refuses to act on a `failed` task and `retryDLQ` doesn't help until the retried task actually completes, a dependent whose dependency dies is blocked forever with no API to resolve it and no signal to the operator. Recommended fix: out of scope for the 12 phases, but worth a plan note — either expose `forceComplete(taskId)` or surface blocked-forever tasks (e.g. a `getBlocked()` helper) so operators can detect the condition. Also, `dependsOn` is never garbage-collected from the `tasks` map, so the map grows without bound for long-lived queues.\n\n### L5. `clonePayload` silently degrades non-plain-object templates\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:558`, `:760-768`.\n\nphase-10 says *\"The `taskTemplate` is used as the payload.\"* The hand-rolled deep clone converts `Date` → plain object, `Map`/`Set` → `{}`, and drops prototypes; verified `payload.d instanceof Date === false` and `payload.nested instanceof Map === false` for a template containing them. Functions survive by reference (they hit the `typeof !== 'object'` branch), which is inconsistent. It also has no cycle detection, so a self-referential template infinite-loops. Recommended fix: use `structuredClone` (Node 17+, and `randomUUID` already assumes modern Node) with a fallback, and note that `structuredClone` throws on functions — which is arguably the correct signal for a task payload.\n\n### L6. Hourly cron skips the repeated hour during DST fall-back\n`/workspace/src/index.ts:747-751`.\n\n`next.setMinutes(0,0,0)` then `next.setHours(date.getHours() + 1)` operates on local wall-clock hours, so during the ambiguous hour the repeated local hour is skipped. Verified in `America/New_York`: from `01:30 EDT` the next fire is `02:00 EST` (90 minutes later), skipping the second `01:00 EST`. Real cron fires both. Daily and `*/N` are correct across both transitions. Low priority since the spec's cron subset is minimal and the tests run in UTC, but worth a comment documenting the local-time semantics. Recommended fix: compute hourly boundaries by UTC epoch arithmetic (`Math.floor(from / HOUR_MS) * HOUR_MS + HOUR_MS`) if UTC semantics are acceptable, matching how the `minutes` branch already works at `:739`.\n\n### L7. Minor spec/API inconsistencies\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:201-204` — `maxRetries: 2.5` is accepted verbatim; `Number.isInteger` check would match the priority validation rigor. `backoffMs: Infinity` also passes and yields an `Invalid Date` for `availableAt`.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:340` — `backoffMs * 2^attempts` overflows to `Infinity` around attempt ~1070, producing `availableAt` = `Invalid Date`; comparisons against `NaN` are always false so the task becomes permanently un-dequeuable. Clamp: `Math.min(backoff, MAX_BACKOFF_MS)`.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:461-481` — `updateProgress` accepts `pending`, `cancelled`, and `completed` tasks (verified progress recorded at 100% on a cancelled task); phase-09 says *\"Report progress for a processing task.\"*\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:474-479` — a throwing `onProgress` listener aborts the loop, so later subscribers never fire and the exception propagates to the `updateProgress` caller. Verified: with listeners `[a, b]`, only `a` runs and `updateProgress` throws. Wrap each listener in `try/catch`.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:180-187` — `enqueue('some-string')` treats the single string argument as a *queue name* with `payload: undefined`, silently creating a queue. Ambiguity is inherent to the overload, but phase-01/02 back-compat (`enqueue(payload)`) means a string payload is unreachable. Worth a documented note.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:335` — `task.lastError = error instanceof Error ? error.message : (error as string)` casts an arbitrary value to `string`; verified `fail(id, { code: 500 })` stores an *object* in a field typed `string | undefined`. Use `String(error)` for the non-Error branch.\n- `/workspace/src/index.ts:266-277` — `dequeue` is O(pending) per call with an O(n) `splice`, so draining a queue is O(n²). Fine at test scale; a priority heap or per-priority buckets would be needed for large queues.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended fix order\n\n1. **H4 + H5** — permanent slot leaks that deadlock queues; smallest, safest diffs.\n2. **H2 + H3** — retry/DLQ accounting; fix together since the budget counter choice couples them.\n3. **M1 + M2** — middleware pipeline terminal states (silent completion is data loss).\n4. **H1** — needs a plan decision on the phase-03/phase-04 conflict before coding.\n5. **M3–M8**, then LOW.\n\nAll 90 phase tests pass before and after my inspection; I edited nothing. Note that H1's fix as written above is deliberately behavior-preserving for the existing suite — if you adopt the literal phase-04 reading instead, `tests/phase-08.test.ts` (\"concurrency respects priority ordering\") and `tests/phase-03.test.ts` will need review, which is itself evidence the spec wording should be amended rather than the comparator."}],"totalDurationMs":422524,"totalTokens":89004,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":666,"cache_read_input_tokens":78387,"output_tokens":9949,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":666},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":9949,"cache_read_input_tokens":78387,"cache_creation_input_tokens":666,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":666,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":2,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":21,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
