+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4vgYuzkskrkAk5Qpk8rzR","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clean — all scratch files deleted, `src/` and existing tests untouched, 90/90 still pass.\n\n# Adversarial review of `/workspace/src/index.ts`\n\nAll findings below were reproduced against the real implementation. Scratch tests have been deleted; `src/` and `tests/` are unmodified (90/90 still pass).\n\n## HIGH — real bugs\n\n**H1. `tick()` throws `RangeError` (stack overflow) on a self-referential schedule template.** `clonePayload` has no cycle tracking (`src/index.ts:760-768`). One bad schedule takes down every subsequent `tick()` permanently — the schedule can never be reached to be unscheduled by that path.\n```ts\nconst tpl: any = { a: 1 }; tpl.self = tpl;\nq.schedule('w', '*/1 * * * *', tpl);\nvi.advanceTimersByTime(120_000);\nq.tick(); // RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded\n```\nExpected: clone handles cycles, or `tick()` isolates per-schedule failures.\n\n**H2. `fail()` on a *pending* (never-dequeued) task grants unlimited retries — task can never reach the DLQ.** `fail()` accepts `status === 'pending'` (`index.ts:327`) and computes `retriesUsed = max(0, attempts - 1)`; a pending task has `attempts === 0`, so `retriesUsed` is always `0 < maxRetries`.\n```ts\nconst t = q.enqueue('w', 1, { maxRetries: 1, backoffMs: 0 });\nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) q.fail(t.id, 'e');\n// failCount === 100, status 'pending', DLQ empty — forever\n```\nExpected: `fail()` on a non-processing task should either reject, or count against `maxRetries`. (Phase 5/6 say \"mark a *processing* task as failed\" and that exhausted retries go to the DLQ.)\n\n**H3. Backoff overflow yields `Invalid Date`, which `isAvailable` treats as \"available now\" — backoff is silently discarded.** `index.ts:340` computes `backoffMs * 2^(attempts-1)`; once it exceeds `~8.64e15`, `new Date(...)` is invalid, and `isAvailable`'s `availableAt.getTime() > now` is `NaN > now === false` (`index.ts:286`).\n```ts\nconst t = q.enqueue('w', 1, { maxRetries: 3, backoffMs: 1e308 });\nq.fail(q.dequeue('w')!.id);\nq.dequeue('w'); // non-null — retried instantly despite a 1e308ms backoff\n```\nAlso reachable organically: with the default `backoffMs: 1000` and `maxRetries` > ~1050. Expected: clamp the backoff (and generally reject/clamp non-finite `availableAt`).\n\n**H4. Concurrency slot permanently stranded because `dequeue()` hands back a live internal object.** `complete()/fail()/cancel()` all early-return when `status` isn't `pending`/`processing` (`index.ts:307`, `:327`, `:505`) *before* doing `queue.processing.delete(...)`. Any caller mutation of `task.status` (or a middleware/handler that sets it) leaks the slot forever.\n```ts\nq.setConcurrency('w', 1);\nq.enqueue('w', 1); q.enqueue('w', 2);\nconst d = q.dequeue('w')!;\nd.status = 'completed';\nq.complete(d.id);                       // early-returns, no slot release\nq.getConcurrency('w');                  // { current: 1, max: 1 }\nq.dequeue('w');                         // null — queue stalled permanently\n```\nExpected: the `processing` set should be reconciled (or tasks returned as copies) so a slot can't be orphaned.\n\n**H5. `enqueue(stringPayload)` silently drops the payload.** The overload disambiguates on `typeof first === 'string'` (`index.ts:180`), so any string payload is read as a queue name. Phase 1/2 mandate `enqueue(payload)` back-compat; string payloads are a totally normal case and no existing test covers them.\n```ts\nq.enqueue('send-email');\n// -> { queueName: 'send-email', payload: undefined }; q.size() === 0\nq.enqueue('my-payload', { priority: 1 });\n// -> queueName 'my-payload', payload === { priority: 1 }, priority 5 (options eaten as payload)\n```\nExpected: at minimum, treat a 2nd arg that looks like `EnqueueOptions` (or `arguments.length === 1`) as the payload form, or document that string payloads are unsupported.\n\n## MEDIUM — real bugs\n\n**M1. `shutdown()` summary undercounts: explicit `cancel()` during shutdown is invisible.** `cancelled` is only incremented in `forceCancelProcessing` (`index.ts:682`); `cancel()` calls `maybeFinishShutdown()` but never bumps the counter (`index.ts:512`).\n```ts\nq.enqueue('w', 1); const d = q.dequeue('w')!;\nconst sp = q.shutdown(5000);\nq.cancel(d.id);\nawait sp; // { completed: 0, cancelled: 0 } while d.status === 'cancelled'\n```\n\n**M2. `fail()`-with-retry during shutdown orphans the task and reports a clean shutdown.** The task returns to `pending`, `countProcessing()` hits 0, shutdown resolves `{completed:0, cancelled:0}` — but `dequeue()` now permanently returns `null` because `shuttingDown` is set (`index.ts:256`). The task is unrunnable and unaccounted for.\n```ts\nq.enqueue('w', 1, { maxRetries: 3, backoffMs: 100 });\nconst d = q.dequeue('w')!;\nconst sp = q.shutdown(5000);\nq.fail(d.id, 'transient');\nawait sp;               // { completed: 0, cancelled: 0 }\nd.status;                // 'pending'\nq.queueSize('w');        // 1, but q.dequeue('w') === null forever\n```\n\n**M3. `tick()` after shutdown throws mid-loop and silently burns a schedule occurrence.** `tick()` mutates `entry.nextRunAt` *before* calling `enqueue()` (`index.ts:557-558`), which throws post-shutdown. The first due schedule loses a run; every later schedule is never examined. Same non-atomicity applies to any `enqueue` throw inside `tick`.\n```ts\nconst s1 = q.schedule('w','*/1 * * * *',{a:1}), s2 = q.schedule('w','*/1 * * * *',{b:2});\nvi.advanceTimersByTime(120_000);\nawait q.shutdown(10);\nq.tick(); // throws \"shutting down\"; s1.nextRunAt already advanced, s2 untouched\n```\n\n**M4. `retryDLQ()` bypasses the shutdown gate.** No `shuttingDown` check (`index.ts:369`), so tasks can be pushed back into `pending` after shutdown resolved, even though `enqueue()` throws.\n```ts\nawait q.shutdown(10);\nq.enqueue('w', 2);      // throws (correct)\nq.retryDLQ(dlqTaskId);  // succeeds, queueSize('w') === 1\n```\n\n**M5. `shutdown()` does not await async `onShutdown` callbacks, and swallows nothing for async throws.** Callbacks are fired with `void callback()` and the `try/catch` only catches *synchronous* throws (`index.ts:642-648`). Two consequences: (a) shutdown resolves before async hooks finish (flush/drain hooks can't complete); (b) a rejecting async hook produces a genuine process-level `unhandledRejection` (observed via Vitest \"Unhandled Errors\" and a `process.on('unhandledRejection')` listener).\n\n**M6. `clonePayload` destroys `Date`/`Map`/`Set`/`RegExp`/class instances in schedule templates** (`index.ts:760`). Every non-plain object becomes `{}`, and prototypes/methods are lost. Phase 10 says \"the `taskTemplate` is used as the payload\"; this quietly corrupts it.\n\n**M7. `fail(id, nonStringNonError)` stores a non-string in `lastError`,** which the `Task` interface types as `string | undefined` (`index.ts:335`). `q.fail(id, { code: 500 })` → `typeof task.lastError === 'object'`, breaking the declared type at runtime.\n\n**M8. `NaN` numeric options are accepted silently.** `maxRetries: NaN`, `backoffMs: NaN`, `delay: NaN` all pass validation (`< 0` is false for `NaN`, `index.ts:202/207/214`). `delay: NaN` produces an `Invalid Date` `availableAt` (dequeued immediately, per H3), and `maxRetries: NaN` makes `retriesUsed < NaN` always false → the task goes straight to the DLQ with zero retries. Note `priority` *is* correctly `Number.isFinite`-checked; the others aren't.\n\n## LOW — real bugs / rough edges\n\n**L1. `complete()` succeeds on a never-dequeued `pending` task** (`index.ts:307`), which unblocks dependents without the task ever having run. Probably lenient by design, but it lets callers fabricate dependency satisfaction; Phase 5 says \"mark a *processing* task as completed.\"\n\n**L2. `unschedule()` returns `true` for an already-inactive schedule.** It only returns `false` for an unknown id (`index.ts:535-540`), so `unschedule(id)` twice returns `true` twice. Entries are also never removed from the map (unbounded growth for long-lived schedulers).\n\n**L3. Fractional concurrency limits are accepted.** `setConcurrency('w', 1.5)` yields an effective limit of 2 (`processing.size >= 1.5`). No integer check (`index.ts:445`).\n\n**L4. `Infinity` priority is rejected but for a confusing reason** — fine — while fractional priority (`5.5`) is accepted. Minor consistency nit only.\n\n## Unspecified behavior / judgment calls (NOT bugs)\n\n- **Delayed vs. priority ordering conflict is a genuine spec contradiction, and the impl picks the other side.** Phase 4 says \"*When multiple delayed tasks are available, they are ordered by their availability time (earliest first), then by priority, then FIFO*\", but `comparePending` sorts priority-first (`index.ts:294-300`). Repro: `delay:100/priority:1` vs `delay:10/priority:9`, advance 500ms → impl returns the priority-1 task; phase-04's literal wording says the earlier-available one. Phase 3's global priority rule supports the impl, and `tests/phase-04.test.ts:59` uses equal priorities so it can't distinguish. **Worth an explicit decision + comment**, not a clear defect.\n- **A cancelled/DLQ'd/cleared dependency blocks the dependent forever.** Explicitly per spec (\"A failed dependency does NOT auto-fail the dependent task - it stays blocked\"). Note the sharpest case: `fail` to DLQ then `clearDLQ` leaves the dependent unblockable with no recovery API. Correct per spec, but worth documenting.\n- **Middleware skipping `next()` marks the task `completed`** (`index.ts:606-608`) even though Phase 11 says \"processing is skipped.\" An auth middleware that rejects a task reports success. Arguably wrong, but the spec doesn't state a terminal status — judgment call.\n- **`next()` called twice is handled correctly.** The `dispatched` guard (`index.ts:585`) prevents double handler invocation; the handler runs exactly once, and the error propagates to `fail()` when uncaught (also for `Promise.all([next(), next()])`). Good.\n- **Pending tasks are left untouched by `shutdown()`** (not cancelled, not counted). Spec only discusses processing tasks. Defensible.\n- **Force-cancelled tasks aren't recounted by a later `complete()`** — verified: returns status `cancelled`, no double-count. Good.\n- **Late `complete()` after shutdown resolves**: `shutdownState` is never nulled, so a stale counter can still be bumped — but `finishShutdown` is guarded by `state.finished`, so the resolved summary can't change. No observable defect.\n- **Cron boundaries verified correct**: `0 * * * *` at exactly `10:00:00` → `11:00`; `0 0 * * *` at exact midnight → next day; month/day rollovers correct; `*/N` for non-divisors of 60 matches real cron (`*/7` at `10:56` → `11:00`); repeated `tick()` at one instant enqueues exactly once; missed periods do not catch up (one task, not N). Correct.\n- **`concurrency: 0` → `Infinity`** matches phase-08's \"`max` should be `Infinity` or `0`\". Fine.\n- **`dequeue()` is an O(n) linear scan**; 5000 tasks dequeue fine. Not a correctness issue."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa8b370c4b6e3a5dc (use SendMessage with to: 'aa8b370c4b6e3a5dc' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 87635\ntool_uses: 24\nduration_ms: 511454</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbaa2cb-485d-41ba-ae15-3484acaf3773","uuid":"74222ccc-92bf-4b75-8b26-add4325d8204","timestamp":"2026-07-25T01:36:53.383Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are an adversarial reviewer. Read /workspace/src/index.ts (a TaskQueue class implementing the 12-phase spec in /workspace/phases/*.md). All 90 tests in /workspace/tests/ pass.\n\nYour job: try to BREAK it. Write throwaway scratch test files (e.g. /tmp/adversarial.test.ts importing from /workspace/src/index.js) and run them with `cd /workspace && npx vitest run /tmp/adversarial.test.ts --root /workspace` (adjust if needed; you may instead create /workspace/tests/tmp-adversarial.test.ts and DELETE it when done — never leave stray files or modify existing tests/src).\n\nProbe scenarios such as:\n- a task whose dependency is later cancelled or fails permanently (does the dependent stay blocked forever? is that per spec?)\n- concurrency slot leaks: cancel a processing task, fail a pending task, complete a pending task, retryDLQ of a task that is currently processing\n- retry after a task was in DLQ then retried then failed repeatedly\n- delayed + priority + concurrency interactions\n- process() with a middleware that calls next() twice, or a handler that throws, or that cancels/completes the task itself\n- shutdown while tasks are being processed via process(); shutdown then complete a task after shutdown resolved (does it throw or miscount?)\n- tick() called repeatedly at the same instant (duplicate enqueues?), tick() after shutdown (enqueue throws inside tick — does it explode?)\n- cron '0 * * * *' and '0 0 * * *' behavior at exact boundaries\n- enqueue backward-compat single-arg form; enqueue with a string payload\n- getProgress/onProgress for unknown task ids; unsubscribe behavior\n- Infinity/0 concurrency semantics\n\nReport a concise list of ACTUAL reproducible defects (with the minimal repro code and observed vs expected behavior), ranked HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW. Distinguish clearly between real bugs and unspecified-behavior judgment calls. Do NOT edit /workspace/src/index.ts or existing tests. Clean up any scratch files you create.","agentId":"aa8b370c4b6e3a5dc","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clean — all scratch files deleted, `src/` and existing tests untouched, 90/90 still pass.\n\n# Adversarial review of `/workspace/src/index.ts`\n\nAll findings below were reproduced against the real implementation. Scratch tests have been deleted; `src/` and `tests/` are unmodified (90/90 still pass).\n\n## HIGH — real bugs\n\n**H1. `tick()` throws `RangeError` (stack overflow) on a self-referential schedule template.** `clonePayload` has no cycle tracking (`src/index.ts:760-768`). One bad schedule takes down every subsequent `tick()` permanently — the schedule can never be reached to be unscheduled by that path.\n```ts\nconst tpl: any = { a: 1 }; tpl.self = tpl;\nq.schedule('w', '*/1 * * * *', tpl);\nvi.advanceTimersByTime(120_000);\nq.tick(); // RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded\n```\nExpected: clone handles cycles, or `tick()` isolates per-schedule failures.\n\n**H2. `fail()` on a *pending* (never-dequeued) task grants unlimited retries — task can never reach the DLQ.** `fail()` accepts `status === 'pending'` (`index.ts:327`) and computes `retriesUsed = max(0, attempts - 1)`; a pending task has `attempts === 0`, so `retriesUsed` is always `0 < maxRetries`.\n```ts\nconst t = q.enqueue('w', 1, { maxRetries: 1, backoffMs: 0 });\nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) q.fail(t.id, 'e');\n// failCount === 100, status 'pending', DLQ empty — forever\n```\nExpected: `fail()` on a non-processing task should either reject, or count against `maxRetries`. (Phase 5/6 say \"mark a *processing* task as failed\" and that exhausted retries go to the DLQ.)\n\n**H3. Backoff overflow yields `Invalid Date`, which `isAvailable` treats as \"available now\" — backoff is silently discarded.** `index.ts:340` computes `backoffMs * 2^(attempts-1)`; once it exceeds `~8.64e15`, `new Date(...)` is invalid, and `isAvailable`'s `availableAt.getTime() > now` is `NaN > now === false` (`index.ts:286`).\n```ts\nconst t = q.enqueue('w', 1, { maxRetries: 3, backoffMs: 1e308 });\nq.fail(q.dequeue('w')!.id);\nq.dequeue('w'); // non-null — retried instantly despite a 1e308ms backoff\n```\nAlso reachable organically: with the default `backoffMs: 1000` and `maxRetries` > ~1050. Expected: clamp the backoff (and generally reject/clamp non-finite `availableAt`).\n\n**H4. Concurrency slot permanently stranded because `dequeue()` hands back a live internal object.** `complete()/fail()/cancel()` all early-return when `status` isn't `pending`/`processing` (`index.ts:307`, `:327`, `:505`) *before* doing `queue.processing.delete(...)`. Any caller mutation of `task.status` (or a middleware/handler that sets it) leaks the slot forever.\n```ts\nq.setConcurrency('w', 1);\nq.enqueue('w', 1); q.enqueue('w', 2);\nconst d = q.dequeue('w')!;\nd.status = 'completed';\nq.complete(d.id);                       // early-returns, no slot release\nq.getConcurrency('w');                  // { current: 1, max: 1 }\nq.dequeue('w');                         // null — queue stalled permanently\n```\nExpected: the `processing` set should be reconciled (or tasks returned as copies) so a slot can't be orphaned.\n\n**H5. `enqueue(stringPayload)` silently drops the payload.** The overload disambiguates on `typeof first === 'string'` (`index.ts:180`), so any string payload is read as a queue name. Phase 1/2 mandate `enqueue(payload)` back-compat; string payloads are a totally normal case and no existing test covers them.\n```ts\nq.enqueue('send-email');\n// -> { queueName: 'send-email', payload: undefined }; q.size() === 0\nq.enqueue('my-payload', { priority: 1 });\n// -> queueName 'my-payload', payload === { priority: 1 }, priority 5 (options eaten as payload)\n```\nExpected: at minimum, treat a 2nd arg that looks like `EnqueueOptions` (or `arguments.length === 1`) as the payload form, or document that string payloads are unsupported.\n\n## MEDIUM — real bugs\n\n**M1. `shutdown()` summary undercounts: explicit `cancel()` during shutdown is invisible.** `cancelled` is only incremented in `forceCancelProcessing` (`index.ts:682`); `cancel()` calls `maybeFinishShutdown()` but never bumps the counter (`index.ts:512`).\n```ts\nq.enqueue('w', 1); const d = q.dequeue('w')!;\nconst sp = q.shutdown(5000);\nq.cancel(d.id);\nawait sp; // { completed: 0, cancelled: 0 } while d.status === 'cancelled'\n```\n\n**M2. `fail()`-with-retry during shutdown orphans the task and reports a clean shutdown.** The task returns to `pending`, `countProcessing()` hits 0, shutdown resolves `{completed:0, cancelled:0}` — but `dequeue()` now permanently returns `null` because `shuttingDown` is set (`index.ts:256`). The task is unrunnable and unaccounted for.\n```ts\nq.enqueue('w', 1, { maxRetries: 3, backoffMs: 100 });\nconst d = q.dequeue('w')!;\nconst sp = q.shutdown(5000);\nq.fail(d.id, 'transient');\nawait sp;               // { completed: 0, cancelled: 0 }\nd.status;                // 'pending'\nq.queueSize('w');        // 1, but q.dequeue('w') === null forever\n```\n\n**M3. `tick()` after shutdown throws mid-loop and silently burns a schedule occurrence.** `tick()` mutates `entry.nextRunAt` *before* calling `enqueue()` (`index.ts:557-558`), which throws post-shutdown. The first due schedule loses a run; every later schedule is never examined. Same non-atomicity applies to any `enqueue` throw inside `tick`.\n```ts\nconst s1 = q.schedule('w','*/1 * * * *',{a:1}), s2 = q.schedule('w','*/1 * * * *',{b:2});\nvi.advanceTimersByTime(120_000);\nawait q.shutdown(10);\nq.tick(); // throws \"shutting down\"; s1.nextRunAt already advanced, s2 untouched\n```\n\n**M4. `retryDLQ()` bypasses the shutdown gate.** No `shuttingDown` check (`index.ts:369`), so tasks can be pushed back into `pending` after shutdown resolved, even though `enqueue()` throws.\n```ts\nawait q.shutdown(10);\nq.enqueue('w', 2);      // throws (correct)\nq.retryDLQ(dlqTaskId);  // succeeds, queueSize('w') === 1\n```\n\n**M5. `shutdown()` does not await async `onShutdown` callbacks, and swallows nothing for async throws.** Callbacks are fired with `void callback()` and the `try/catch` only catches *synchronous* throws (`index.ts:642-648`). Two consequences: (a) shutdown resolves before async hooks finish (flush/drain hooks can't complete); (b) a rejecting async hook produces a genuine process-level `unhandledRejection` (observed via Vitest \"Unhandled Errors\" and a `process.on('unhandledRejection')` listener).\n\n**M6. `clonePayload` destroys `Date`/`Map`/`Set`/`RegExp`/class instances in schedule templates** (`index.ts:760`). Every non-plain object becomes `{}`, and prototypes/methods are lost. Phase 10 says \"the `taskTemplate` is used as the payload\"; this quietly corrupts it.\n\n**M7. `fail(id, nonStringNonError)` stores a non-string in `lastError`,** which the `Task` interface types as `string | undefined` (`index.ts:335`). `q.fail(id, { code: 500 })` → `typeof task.lastError === 'object'`, breaking the declared type at runtime.\n\n**M8. `NaN` numeric options are accepted silently.** `maxRetries: NaN`, `backoffMs: NaN`, `delay: NaN` all pass validation (`< 0` is false for `NaN`, `index.ts:202/207/214`). `delay: NaN` produces an `Invalid Date` `availableAt` (dequeued immediately, per H3), and `maxRetries: NaN` makes `retriesUsed < NaN` always false → the task goes straight to the DLQ with zero retries. Note `priority` *is* correctly `Number.isFinite`-checked; the others aren't.\n\n## LOW — real bugs / rough edges\n\n**L1. `complete()` succeeds on a never-dequeued `pending` task** (`index.ts:307`), which unblocks dependents without the task ever having run. Probably lenient by design, but it lets callers fabricate dependency satisfaction; Phase 5 says \"mark a *processing* task as completed.\"\n\n**L2. `unschedule()` returns `true` for an already-inactive schedule.** It only returns `false` for an unknown id (`index.ts:535-540`), so `unschedule(id)` twice returns `true` twice. Entries are also never removed from the map (unbounded growth for long-lived schedulers).\n\n**L3. Fractional concurrency limits are accepted.** `setConcurrency('w', 1.5)` yields an effective limit of 2 (`processing.size >= 1.5`). No integer check (`index.ts:445`).\n\n**L4. `Infinity` priority is rejected but for a confusing reason** — fine — while fractional priority (`5.5`) is accepted. Minor consistency nit only.\n\n## Unspecified behavior / judgment calls (NOT bugs)\n\n- **Delayed vs. priority ordering conflict is a genuine spec contradiction, and the impl picks the other side.** Phase 4 says \"*When multiple delayed tasks are available, they are ordered by their availability time (earliest first), then by priority, then FIFO*\", but `comparePending` sorts priority-first (`index.ts:294-300`). Repro: `delay:100/priority:1` vs `delay:10/priority:9`, advance 500ms → impl returns the priority-1 task; phase-04's literal wording says the earlier-available one. Phase 3's global priority rule supports the impl, and `tests/phase-04.test.ts:59` uses equal priorities so it can't distinguish. **Worth an explicit decision + comment**, not a clear defect.\n- **A cancelled/DLQ'd/cleared dependency blocks the dependent forever.** Explicitly per spec (\"A failed dependency does NOT auto-fail the dependent task - it stays blocked\"). Note the sharpest case: `fail` to DLQ then `clearDLQ` leaves the dependent unblockable with no recovery API. Correct per spec, but worth documenting.\n- **Middleware skipping `next()` marks the task `completed`** (`index.ts:606-608`) even though Phase 11 says \"processing is skipped.\" An auth middleware that rejects a task reports success. Arguably wrong, but the spec doesn't state a terminal status — judgment call.\n- **`next()` called twice is handled correctly.** The `dispatched` guard (`index.ts:585`) prevents double handler invocation; the handler runs exactly once, and the error propagates to `fail()` when uncaught (also for `Promise.all([next(), next()])`). Good.\n- **Pending tasks are left untouched by `shutdown()`** (not cancelled, not counted). Spec only discusses processing tasks. Defensible.\n- **Force-cancelled tasks aren't recounted by a later `complete()`** — verified: returns status `cancelled`, no double-count. Good.\n- **Late `complete()` after shutdown resolves**: `shutdownState` is never nulled, so a stale counter can still be bumped — but `finishShutdown` is guarded by `state.finished`, so the resolved summary can't change. No observable defect.\n- **Cron boundaries verified correct**: `0 * * * *` at exactly `10:00:00` → `11:00`; `0 0 * * *` at exact midnight → next day; month/day rollovers correct; `*/N` for non-divisors of 60 matches real cron (`*/7` at `10:56` → `11:00`); repeated `tick()` at one instant enqueues exactly once; missed periods do not catch up (one task, not N). Correct.\n- **`concurrency: 0` → `Infinity`** matches phase-08's \"`max` should be `Infinity` or `0`\". Fine.\n- **`dequeue()` is an O(n) linear scan**; 5000 tasks dequeue fine. Not a correctness issue."}],"totalDurationMs":511454,"totalTokens":87635,"totalToolUseCount":24,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":523,"cache_read_input_tokens":82590,"output_tokens":4520,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":523},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4520,"cache_read_input_tokens":82590,"cache_creation_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":523,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":1,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":15,"editFileCount":8,"linesAdded":2258,"linesRemoved":23,"otherToolCount":0}}}
